#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/id #uuid "adb10509-7cc7-58fb-9c2c-5a219d0bec86" :question/math-academy-id "e-12154" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1$. Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - (5^{2x_{n}} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2x_{n}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - (5^{2x_{0}} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2x_{0}}). \\\\\n&= 1 - (5^{2} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2}). \\\\\n&\\approx 0.938\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/id #uuid "35f589f7-0823-50bf-9bee-e2b9202bfda3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12155" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5$. Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{e^{x_{n}} - x_{n}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{n}} - 3x_{n}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{e^{x_{0}} - x_{0}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{0}} - 3x_{0}^{2}} \\\\\n&= 5 - (e^{5} - 5^{3} - 3)/(e^{5} - 3(5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4.721941…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\frac{e^{x_{1}} - x_{1}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{1}} - 3x_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (4.721941…) - (e^{4.721941…} - (4.721941…)^{3} - 3)/(e^{4.721941…} - 3(4.721941…)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 4.632\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "af6352ce-9fb6-5951-962a-8fe316f05c50" :question/math-academy-id "e-12156" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2]$, use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{x_{n}^{5} - 2}{5x_{n}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (x_{n}(5x_{n}^{4}) - (x_{n}^{5} - 2))/(5x_{n}^{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4x_{n}^{5} + 2}{5x_{n}^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\frac{4x_{0}^{5} + 2}{5x_{0}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1)^{5} + 2)/(5(1)^{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5} \\\\\n&= 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= \\frac{4x_{1}^{5} + 2}{5x_{1}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1.2)^{5} + 2)/(5(1.2)^{4}) \\\\\n&= 1.152… \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{4x_{2}^{5} + 2}{5x_{2}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1.152…)^{5} + 2)/(5(1.152…)^{4}) \\\\\n&= 1.148…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations\n\n$$\nx_2 \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_3 \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nare the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c0949f44-7be5-5e14-b9df-8a01aeffcf8c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090167} {:answer-field/id #uuid "68461f14-52c0-5c86-94a8-579d622155a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090168}] :question/id #uuid "55659377-1a68-5cde-bfb5-257e09e1e167" :question/math-academy-id "q-255772" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = \\ln x - 1$ in the interval $[1, 3]$. Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 1.5$. Round your answers to three decimal places.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-a-functions-root-using-the-newton-raphson-method\"></a>" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f27c05ad-39e2-5911-a13f-0a202b1b47b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090170}] :question/id #uuid "d7e60767-88af-589b-a4b8-bb73f6ce04f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-255853" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = x^{2} + x - 3$ in the interval $[0, 2]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 0.8$.$x_{1} =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
